WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
THE GLORY DAYS:
Remember Sprint? 10-15 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:09.480
Sprint was one of the big 3 telecom companies&nbsp;
along with AT&amp;T and Verizon. They had tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.360
millions of subscribers and were pulling in tens&nbsp;
of billions of dollars. But by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:22.040
decade, the picture looked a lot different. Not&nbsp;
only was Sprint bleeding subscribers and revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:28.280
but they were being shown up by a new player: T&nbsp;
Mobile. T Mobile had risen from the ashes of near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:34.240
bankruptcy and was making a name for themselves&nbsp;
as the “uncarrier”. Everything that you hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.440
about cell phone plans and carriers, T&nbsp;
Mobile eliminated. No more contracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.120
no more hidden fees, and no more sleazy salesmen.&nbsp;
And this pitch worked. It worked really well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.680
actually. Within just a few years, T Mobile would&nbsp;
grow from being a distant player behind Sprint to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:57.080
trying to acquire the entirety of Sprint. This&nbsp;
was definitely a punch to the gut for Sprint and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:03.480
likely a deal that they did not want to accept.&nbsp;
But looking inwards at the fundamentals of Sprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.440
it was obvious that T Mobile was making a generous&nbsp;
offer and turning it down would be a disservice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:16.640
shareholders. So, in 2018, Sprint would agree&nbsp;
to a $26.5 billion deal with T Mobile. It took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.240
a few years to actually finalize the deal and get&nbsp;
in through regulators but eventually on April 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.200
2020, T Mobile would complete their acquisition of&nbsp;
Sprint. At first, there was some hope that maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.720
T Mobile would keep around the Sprint branding and&nbsp;
just operate at as a subsidiary like they did with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:40.840
Boost Mobile. But, T Mobile immediately moved to&nbsp;
wipe away all remnants of Sprint. And just a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:47.760
months after the acquisition on August 2, 2020,&nbsp;
the Sprint brand would be officially discontinued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.840
With the help of Sprint, T Mobile has become the&nbsp;
largest telecom company in the US, and revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.840 --> 00:02:00.200
has exploded to a new tier, so things are going&nbsp;
better than ever. But, the same cannot be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.000
about Sprint. If you try accessing Sprint.com,&nbsp;
you’re met with this sad landing page that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.760
redirects you to the T Mobile website. But, how&nbsp;
did this happen? How did Sprint go from being one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.720
of the big 3 telecom companies to being completely&nbsp;
erased off the face of the planet within a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:29.760
10 years? Well, join me, as we take a look back at&nbsp;
the rise and fall of the forgotten giant: Sprint.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.000
THE UNDERDOG:
Sprint was obviously left behind largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.640
because of their unwillingness to change with the&nbsp;
times. Their story is particularly interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.000
because unlike their peers, they didn’t came from&nbsp;
a monopolistic background. If you didn’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.880
AT&amp;T and Verizon both evolved from the original&nbsp;
AT&amp;T which at their peak controlled 90% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:57.200
US telephone business. Similarly, T Mobile has&nbsp;
very much benefitted from the backing of Deutsche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.960
Telecom, Germany’s national telecom carrier.&nbsp;
Sprint, however, has always been the underdog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.920
as the story of Sprint dates back to 1899 to&nbsp;
the rural town of Abeline, Kansas to a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:16.000
named Cleyson Leroy Brown. Cleyson was the sone&nbsp;
of Jacob Brown, a local grist mill owner. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.760
this would’ve led to a pretty nice upbringing&nbsp;
but unfortunately for Cleyson, his father’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.640
mill turned out to be his worst nightmare as&nbsp;
he would lose his arm to a mill accident when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:31.320
he was just 10 years old. Cleyson didn’t let this&nbsp;
accident wear him down though. He would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.520
be a teacher and a manager at a creamery before&nbsp;
opening a local electric company that generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.080
electricity using the Smoky Hill River. It was&nbsp;
around this time that telephones first started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.760
make their appearance across the nation, and when&nbsp;
it came to telephones, there was only one option:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.000
the Bell telephone company or AT&amp;T. The local&nbsp;
people of Abeline weren’t exactly a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.720
this as they much preferred supporting a local&nbsp;
business man as opposed to the national giant. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.920
at their request, Cleyson would establish&nbsp;
the Brown Telephone Company in 1899, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.720
brings us into the first key strategy of Sprint,&nbsp;
customer loyalty and connection. The reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.560
the Brown Telephone Company was successful was&nbsp;
not because they had insane resources or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.880
sort of groundbreaking technology. No, the reason&nbsp;
that the Brown Telephone Company was successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.200
was because they had a personal connection with&nbsp;
their customers and their customers were rooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.400
for their victory. It was this same strategy&nbsp;
that T Mobile capitalized on over a 100 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.440
later with their whole “uncarrier” movement.&nbsp;
Customers were rooting for T Mobile to redefine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.240
the carrier landscape and clean the space. The&nbsp;
same, however, cannot be said about the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:45.160
Sprint. Most customers didn’t have a particularly&nbsp;
positive or negative view on sprint but they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.480
only using Sprint for one main reason: because&nbsp;
it made financial sense. Sprint gave them a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:57.200
good deal or a strong discount which got them in&nbsp;
the door. When that initial promo ended though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:03.440
people would simply leave. In other words,&nbsp;
Sprint had very little customer loyalty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.680
this really hurt them. All of the new customers&nbsp;
that they were getting were money losers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.266
and right when they tried to convert them into&nbsp;
profitable customers, they would just leave. Aka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.266 --> 00:05:14.480
each new customer was a net negative for the&nbsp;
company. The best way to overcome this pitfall is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.920
to give customers a reason to stick around that’s&nbsp;
not just purely financial or logical similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.920
what T Mobile or the Brown Telephone Company did.&nbsp;
Without this X factor though, Sprint simply became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:38.747
the place that savvy customers farmed deals&nbsp;
which eventually made the company unviable.

00:05:38.747 --> 00:05:40.960
BOTCHED DEAL:
Fortunately, things were looking much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.400
for Cleyson Brown back in the day. Cleyson never&nbsp;
had aspirations of growing some sort of national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.000
giant. In fact, he much preferred to run a variety&nbsp;
of different local businesses. After founding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Brown Telephone Company, Cleyson opened up shoe&nbsp;
stores, grocery stores, a gravel and sand company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.200
hotels, a news service, a broadcast station,&nbsp;
insurance companies, and oil companies. As you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:09.240
can see, Cleyson was much more of a multi faceted&nbsp;
business man as opposed to being a magnate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.680
any one sector. But, this didn’t mean that he&nbsp;
squandered growth opportunities that did came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:20.880
his way. In fact he was very much known for his&nbsp;
consolidation and expansion skills. In 1911 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.120
example, he merged the Brown Telephone company&nbsp;
with three other local telephone companies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.400
create the United Telephone Company. And in 1925,&nbsp;
he would further consolidate to create the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:36.240
Telephone and Electric Company. Sprint very&nbsp;
much kept up this tradition of strong mergers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.880
and acquisitions well past Cleyson’s days and&nbsp;
this brings us into their second key strategy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:47.240
strategic deals. This is precisely how Sprint&nbsp;
grew from being a regional phone business to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:54.040
being a national giant: consistently making&nbsp;
better and better deals. For example, in 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:59.960
Brown’s company would make a ground breaking deal&nbsp;
with Southern Pacific Communications or SPC. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:05.240
would agree to merge with SPC and create a new&nbsp;
joint venture that called Sprint which stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:11.600
for Southern Pacific Railroad Internal Networking&nbsp;
Telephony. Similarly, in 1993, Sprint would merge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:17.640
with a cellular business called Cental which gave&nbsp;
them access to the wireless market. And in 1996,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:22.840
they would strike a deal with Radioshack to open&nbsp;
up Sprint stores inside Radioshacks. Those were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:28.520
just a few of Sprint’s most notable deals. They’ve&nbsp;
completed dozens of such deals and that’s how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:34.160
really grew their national presence, but not&nbsp;
all of their deals had such a happy ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.840
which brings us into their deal with Nextel&nbsp;
Communications. In 2004, Sprint announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.240
they would be merging with Nextel Communications.&nbsp;
At the time, Sprint was the 3rd largest player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:51.000
and Nextel was the 5th largest player within the&nbsp;
mobile phone industry. So, on paper, it seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:58.440
that this merger would make this duo unstoppable,&nbsp;
but see, there’s more to a merger than just size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.320
For a merger to be successful, both parties need&nbsp;
to have significant synergies that can benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:08.600
from each other. For example, maybe Sprint had&nbsp;
strong network in the east and Nextel had a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:15.280
network in the west. Together, they could have&nbsp;
a strong nationwide network, but that’s not how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:21.480
things quite played out. You see, not only did&nbsp;
Sprint and Nextel not have many synergies but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.680
actually had a bunch of opposing technologies&nbsp;
that could not be combined. As such, if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.600
new company wanted to streamline and consolidate&nbsp;
their networks, they often had to choose between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.880
the two companies' holdings rendering the merger&nbsp;
completely useless. To make things worse, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.680
integration efforts often led to outages and cell&nbsp;
phone plans simply not working altogether. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:47.400
not only did the merger not allow them to reach&nbsp;
new heights but it made them look bad in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:53.240
their existing customers. Sprint would eventually&nbsp;
write off almost the entirety of Nextel in 2008 as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.240 --> 00:09:00.520
a $29.5 billion loss and they would shut down&nbsp;
the company altogether in 2013. Having wasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:06.560
a full decade and tens of billions on this deal,&nbsp;
Sprint had fallen far behind AT&amp;T and Verizon when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:19.110
it came to network coverage and reliability,&nbsp;
and this leads us into their eventual demise.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.960
EGO KILLS:
By the time that Sprint shut down Nextel, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.680
were looking pretty bleak. They had a subpar&nbsp;
network with bruised morale, unloyal customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.200
and an empty balance sheet. But, honestly, you&nbsp;
could argue that T Mobile was in an even worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:39.840
position. In fact, T Mobile wasn’t even allowed to&nbsp;
sell iPhones till 2013, yet they were able do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:46.200
over the next 10 years, so Sprint was by no means&nbsp;
a lost cause. They definitely still had a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:53.280
to recover but staging such a recovery requires&nbsp;
one key characteristic: humility. This was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.840
of the strong suits of Cleyson. He didn’t live&nbsp;
a particularly long life having passed away at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.560
age 63, but despite that, he spent the latter&nbsp;
half of his life giving back to the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:10.280
that had given him everything. In 1926, he built&nbsp;
a Memorial Foundation in memory of his parents. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:15.440
would also go onto to build a 200 acre park for&nbsp;
free which included an amusement park, a lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:21.480
a zoo, a golf course, tennis courts, and more.&nbsp;
Of course, this has no direct link to business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:27.480
but at the same time, it does. Humility is&nbsp;
extremely important when it comes to running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.000
a business. It turns out that customers are&nbsp;
surprisingly understanding when you’re honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.240
about a shortcoming instead of trying to hide it&nbsp;
or deceive them about it. Again, this is precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:42.360
what T Mobile leveraged. They admitted that&nbsp;
they sucked. They didn’t have the best service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:47.280
that was simply a fact. But, they were working&nbsp;
on fixing this and while they couldn’t promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.480
you the best service right now, what they&nbsp;
could promise you was an honest transparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:56.240
relationship, something that you couldn’t get from&nbsp;
the big players. It would’ve been in Sprint’s best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.840
interest to leverage a similar strategy but&nbsp;
their ego rendered this virtually impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:07.160
Sprint approached the situation with the ego of&nbsp;
a national giant. As far as they were concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:11.880
they were still the big dog and they were&nbsp;
just facing a temporary downfall. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:18.200
they thought they could just pretend that they&nbsp;
weren’t inferior to AT&amp;T and Verizon and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:23.520
incentivize themselves out of this situation.&nbsp;
You know, by offering strong discounts and deals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.920
but as we discussed, this just led to net&nbsp;
negative customers who were only there for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:35.040
the incentive which is not really surprising.&nbsp;
After all, we’re talking about a carrier that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.240
came with all of the same shortfalls as AT&amp;T&nbsp;
and Verizon. You know, the 2 year contracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:46.320
the hidden fees, and the sleazy sales people. But,&nbsp;
the network wasn’t even good either meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:52.720
the only reason to stick around was for financial&nbsp;
incentives. So, trying to buy customers wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:59.920
exactly a great long term strategy. To make&nbsp;
things worse, given that Sprint never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:04.120
admitted that they had a problem, they never&nbsp;
addressed their shortcomings. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:08.560
they decided against building traditional&nbsp;
cell towers for some reason. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:13.720
they were relatively late to 5G, something&nbsp;
that T Mobile was early to. And when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:17.320
combine this lack of customer loyalty&nbsp;
with a botched deal that’s weighing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.120
the company and a general unwillingness&nbsp;
to admit shortcomings and overcome them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.960
Sprint’s fate was sealed long before T Mobile&nbsp;
actually took over and put them down for good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:31.520
If you would like to see how T Mobile managed to&nbsp;
do what Sprint couldn’t, check out this video.

